  "worked_solution": "First, we can simplify our expression by factoring it as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}\u2061x-{tan}^{4}\u2061x & =({sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x)({sec}^{2}\u2061x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x)({sec}^{2}\u2061x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x) & =(1)({sec}^{2}\u2061x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}\u2061x-{tan}^{4}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$",
